            0.443
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ready to Go Beyond"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.162,
            0.45,
            0.413,
            0.468
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Airplane fuel miscalculation"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.159,
            0.469,
            0.929,
            0.565
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "In a well-known incident, a passenger aircraft ran out of fuel mid-flight due to a mix-up in units. The flight needed 22,300 kg fuel in total, but the ground crew miscalculated the fuel required, since they used the density of fuel in pounds (lb) per litre rather than kilograms (kg) per litre. The aircraft was about 15,000 litres short of fuel and luckily could glide to an emergency landing, which damaged the aircraft though there were no casualties. Pounds and kilograms are very different. Using standard (SI) units everywhere avoids conversions and errors."
            }
          ]
        },
